# Copy to `.env` and fill in what is missing. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not,
# so it must never hold a real value.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#
# Every value below is already the default, so a copied file changes nothing until you
# edit it. Only HF_TOKEN is blank, and the recommended setup leaves it that way.
#
# Nothing in the package loads this file: these are environment variables, and the
# file records which ones exist, what each is for, and what happens when it is unset.
# It is written to be sourced by bash, which is why `$HOME` appears unquoted -- a
# dotenv parser that does no variable expansion would take that literally.
#
# Pharos generates, gates, and runs every model-free measurement with none of it set.

# --- Hugging Face -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Only needed to fetch model weights, which `scripts/train_adapter.py` and the cluster
# jobs do. Everything Pharos downloads is public, so an unauthenticated run works --
# it is merely rate limited, and a throttled download inside a GPU allocation burns
# the allocation rather than failing fast. A 24-task array is 24 chances to hit that.
#
# Prefer `hf auth login` and leave this blank. It writes `$HF_HOME/token` at mode 0600,
# whereas a variable is inherited by every child process and survives in shell history,
# in `ps e`, and in any crash dump that captures the environment. Set it here only for
# a context that cannot run an interactive login, such as CI.
#
# Fine-grained, READ-only. Pharos never uploads, so a write-scoped token grants
# strictly more than it needs.
HF_TOKEN=

# Where model weights are cached, and where `hf auth login` puts the token. The default
# is correct on both sides. What it must not become on the cluster is a node-local temp
# dir: the shared home filesystem is what stops every job in an array refetching several
# gigabytes an earlier task already paid for. Every sbatch file exports this same
# default, so overriding it here without overriding it there splits the cache in two.
HF_HOME=$HOME/.cache/huggingface

# --- Observability ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset -- the default -- every span is a no-op and measurements still emit structured
# JSON on stdout. Set, traces and metrics are exported. What degrades without this is
# the export, never the number.
#
# `docker compose up -d` brings up the collector, Jaeger and Prometheus this points at.
# The port is the OTLP-over-HTTP one, because `pharos.telemetry` imports the HTTP
# exporters; 4317 is the gRPC port the collector also listens on and nothing here
# speaks. Uncomment after the collector is up: pointing at a closed port makes every
# export attempt fail in the background.
# PHAROS_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318

# INFO is the default and the right one for a sweep. DEBUG additionally surfaces the
# routine per-item metrics -- `record_routine` exists so a thirty-corpus sweep does not
# bury its own result under thirty identical lines, and DEBUG is how you get them back
# when a single corpus is the thing under investigation. `scripts/logcheck.py` sets this
# per-subprocess, so it is unaffected by whatever is set here.
PHAROS_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- Threads ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset locally: BLAS should use the whole machine. On a shared node it must be set,
# and to `$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK` rather than a number, because numpy otherwise sizes its
# pool from the machine's core count and not the cores the allocation actually got --
# a 4-CPU task spawning 64 threads, contending with every other job on the node.
#
# `pharos.telemetry` records all four in every artifact and raises `oversubscription_risk`
# when usable CPUs are below machine CPUs and none of them is capped, so a run that hit
# this is identifiable after the fact rather than merely slow. The sbatch files set them.
# OMP_NUM_THREADS=$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK
# OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK
# MKL_NUM_THREADS=$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK
# NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS=$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK

# --- Cluster ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Overrides for `scripts/sync_cluster.sh`, which fetches a commit rather than copying
# files so artifacts produced there can name the code that produced them. The host is
# an ssh alias, so the defaults work once `~/.ssh/config` has one -- see `cluster/`.
PHAROS_CLUSTER_HOST=sporc
PHAROS_CLUSTER_PATH=$HOME/ajsoftworks/pharos

# --- Cluster jobs -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by `cluster/adapter.sbatch` and `cluster/review-adapter.sbatch`, which are
# submitted from a cluster shell, so setting these locally does nothing. They are here
# because the defaults are the published configuration and changing one silently makes
# a run non-comparable with the artifacts already in `results/`.
#
# The base model every adapter is trained from. Ungated and Apache-2.0, so it needs no
# license acceptance and no gated-repo permission on the token.
PHAROS_ADAPTER_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct

# The four named teachers. The 24-point grid overrides this with `t<threshold>s<slip>`
# specs instead, which is what `--array=0-23%4` sweeps. Quoted because this file is
# sourced and the value has spaces; the sbatch expands it unquoted on purpose, so the
# four names word-split back into four arguments.
PHAROS_REVIEWERS="by-the-book inattentive two-of-three any-one"

# Held-out evaluation size, and the corpus generated to supply it. 600 is not
# arbitrary: `scripts/measure_power.py` puts the minimum detectable effect at 60 tasks
# at 0.233, which cannot resolve the 0.083 gap between the careless teacher's adapter
# and that teacher, and at 600 it is 0.070, which can. EVENTS tracks it so that
# EVENTS - EVAL_TASKS stays at 1140 and every run trains on the same amount of data
# as the first sweep. Lower EVAL_TASKS and the comparison stops being clean twice over.
PHAROS_EVAL_TASKS=600
PHAROS_EVENTS=1740

# Unset -- the default -- training and evaluation split one corpus by event, showing
# the rule generalises across events. Set to a different seed, they share no corpus at
# all: different vessels, different events, different renderings. That is the stronger
# claim, and what contamination-resistance guidance asks of a benchmark. Artifacts from
# such a run are suffixed `-xseed<N>` so they never overwrite the in-corpus ones.
# PHAROS_EVAL_SEED=

# --- Deliberately absent from this file -------------------------------------------
# PHAROS_PUSH_OVER_RED disables the pre-push hook that refuses to push on top of a red
# CI run. Do not put it here. Sourcing `.env` would turn the hook off permanently and
# silently, which is exactly the failure it exists to catch: four commits in this
# repository's history failed the same single test, each pushed without anyone reading
# the result of the one before. Pushing a fix for a red run is the normal case and is
# allowed by saying so once, on the command line that does it:
#
#     PHAROS_PUSH_OVER_RED=1 git push
#
# The override is the point. What went wrong was never pushing onto red; it was pushing
# onto red without knowing. A variable in a file you sourced hours ago is not knowing.

# --- Not an environment variable --------------------------------------------------
# The model endpoint is `--endpoint`, a flag on every measurement that calls a model,
# defaulting to http://localhost:11434/api/generate. There is deliberately no variable
# for it: which endpoint produced a number is an argument the artifact records, not
# ambient state a shell can change without the artifact noticing.
